proof.text:
Write $J=\mathbf N^n$ and $f=(c_
u)_{
u\in J}$ using [def-trf-restricted-series-presentation]. The finite set $S=\{
u:|c_
u|\ge1\}$ gives the bound $|c_
u|\le\max(1,\max_{\mu\in S}|c_\mu|)$ (take the finite maximum to be zero when $S$ is empty). Thus the supremum is finite and bounds each coefficient. Its vanishing is equivalent to all coefficients being zero. The coefficient formulas for $1,-f,f+g$, together with $|1|=1$, $|-a|=|a|$ and the assumed ultrametric inequality, give $\|1\|=1$, $\|-f\|=\|f\|$ and $\|f+g\|\le\max(\|f\|,\|g\|)$. The same coefficient inequalities show the sum is restricted: its $\epsilon$-exceptional set is contained in the union of the two finite exceptional sets. Consequently $d(f,g)=\|f-g\|$ is a metric, with its triangle inequality following from the stronger maximum bound.
